    "q-158560": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158560",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{x-y}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4>0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\ge y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge y,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-158560-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne y,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\}",
